      "text": "We begin by setting up the conditions given in the problem using modular arithmetic. The first two conditions give us the following congruences:\n\n1. When the coins are divided among six people, four coins are left over. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Coins} \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n\n2. When the coins are divided among five people, three coins are left over. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Coins} \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive integer \\( x \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's denote \\( x \\) by the number of coins. We need to solve the system of congruences:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we express \\( x \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nx = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = 6k + 4 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\quad \\text{(since } -1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\text{)}\n\\]\n\nReduce 6 modulo 5:\n\\[\n6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSo the congruence simplifies to:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nThis means:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into \\( x = 6k + 4 \\):\n\\[\nx = 6(5m + 4) + 4\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 30m + 24 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 30m + 28\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( x \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\):\n\\[\nx = 28\n\\]\n\nSo, the number of coins in the box is 28. Now, we need to determine how many coins are left when 28 coins are divided among seven people:\n\\[\n28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of } 0\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of coins left when 28 coins are divided among seven people is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
