      "text": "Let's denote the number of coins in the box as \\( N \\).\n\nFrom the problem, we have the following conditions:\n1. When \\( N \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 4. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n2. When \\( N \\) is divided by 5, the remainder is 3. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive integer \\( N \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nN = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\nSubstitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSimplify the left side:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[\nk \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), we have:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\nSubstitute this expression back into the expression for \\( N \\):\n\\[\nN = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28\n\\]\nSo, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nN = 30m + 28\n\\]\nWe need to find the smallest positive \\( N \\). If we set \\( m = 0 \\):\n\\[\nN = 30(0) + 28 = 28\n\\]\nWe need to verify that 28 satisfies both original conditions:\n1. \\( 28 \\div 6 = 4 \\) remainder \\( 4 \\), so \\( 28 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\)\n2. \\( 28 \\div 5 = 5 \\) remainder \\( 3 \\), so \\( 28 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nSince both conditions are satisfied, the smallest number of coins is 28. Now, we need to find how many coins are left when 28 is divided by 7:\n\\[\n28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 0\n\\]\nSo, the number of coins left when equally divided among seven people is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
