      "text": "Let's denote the number of coins in the box as \\( N \\). We are given two conditions related to \\( N \\):\n\n1. When \\( N \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 4.\n2. When \\( N \\) is divided by 5, the remainder is 3.\n\nWe can express these conditions using congruences:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nFirst, let's express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side modulo 5, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we get:\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1\\) is congruent to 4 modulo 5, we have:\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[ k = 5m + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[ N = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nSo, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[ N = 30m + 28 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). The smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), giving us:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 28 is divided by 7:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\]\nSo, the remainder is:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ with a remainder of } 0 \\]\nTherefore, the number of coins left when 28 is equally divided among seven people is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
